 "body": "## Design\n\nThe owner's answer R1 of 2026-11-01 on how a pattern is written in a literal, given through the question tool on #176 (comment 6560564782), is recorded in `kurz-design.md`, section 8. It comes with what it was chosen against and its cost, as the options named them:\n\n- R1 \"Rust's raw r\\\"...\\\"\": `r\"\\d{2,4}\"` holds its text as written, with no escape and no interpolation. `r#\"...\"#` holds a `\"`, and a raw `\"\"\"` block stands beside it.\n  - It was chosen against C#'s verbatim literal `@\"...\"`, at the same cost in C#'s spelling.\n  - It was chosen against a pattern literal of its own, `/\\d{2,4}/`, at the cost of a third kind of literal and a `/` read as division beside it.\n  - It was chosen against the escapes as they are, at the cost of every class and every brace doubled in every pattern and every JSON text.\n  - Its cost: a second kind of literal in the lexer, and a reader must know which form keeps escapes.\n- This revisits the owner's answer of 2026-10-02 (escapes and interpolation in every literal, the `\"\"\"` block too) for the raw forms alone. As the answer on #176 says, that answer stands for every literal that is not raw. Section 8's \"String interpolation is always on\" and its sentence on escapes now say \"not raw\".\n\nMarked *(assumed)*, proposed with this record and not put to the owner:\n- The raw block opens with `r\"\"\"` at the end of a line and takes every rule of the `\"\"\"` block (L13): its line ends, its indentation and its shapes.\n- `r#\"` takes one to 255 `#` and closes at a `\"` followed by as many, as Rust's lexer counts them.\n- A raw literal ends on the line it starts on, as an ordinary literal does, where Rust's runs over lines, since the raw block holds several lines.\n- A raw block has no `#` form, as the block has no form of four quotes.\n- An `r` directly before `\"`, or before `#` and a `\"`, starts a raw literal wherever a name could start. A name `r` followed by a string was text no rule gives a meaning before.\n- A raw literal that does not end on its line is the error an ordinary one is, `syntax`, and a raw block of a wrong shape gets the error a block of that shape gets.\n- A raw literal stands wherever a literal does, an interpolation's hole and an `asm` template among them. In a template, its `{name}` names an operand, as in any template.\n\n## Reference and corpus\n\n- L20 (decided, \u00a78) in chapter 1, with six cases under `corpus/source/raw-*.kz`:\n  - the one-line form, with `\\`, `{` and the `#` forms;\n  - a raw block;\n  - a raw literal in an interpolation's hole, whose `\\` would otherwise end the hole wrongly;\n  - a literal that does not close on its line;\n  - a `#` form whose closing is missing;\n  - a raw block on one line.\n- L6 (title and text), L7 and L15 now say \"not raw\". The `syntax` row of `reference/12-errors.md` names L20.\n\n## Compiler\n\n- The lexer reads the three forms (`Lexer.cs`: `LexRawString`, and `LexBlockString` with `raw`, which keeps L13's indentation and line ends and appends each line's text as written). It refuses a literal that does not close on its line and more than 255 `#` as `syntax`.\n- A raw literal in a hole is passed over at its own quote (`SkipNestedRawLiteral`, in `ReadHole` and in `SkipHole`).\n- `LexerTests` gained 15 cases, two of them raw templates of `asm`.\n- Each change was seen red:\n  - without the dispatch, the three output cases and the lexer cases of each form fail;\n  - without the skip in the hole, `raw-in-interpolation.kz` and its lexer case fail;\n  - with the raw block reading escapes, `raw-block.kz` and two lexer cases fail;\n  - without the bound of 255, its lexer case fails.\n\n## Research findings (node B4)\n\nNone is met. Rust 15 (too many string types) is not repeated: a raw literal is a second spelling of `string`, not a type.\n\nNo gate, tool, hook, workflow or review rule changes, so neither `knowledge/diagram-gate-map.md` nor `guides/quality-bar.md` applies.\n\n## Checks\n\n- `python3 tools/gates.py` on fb0503f, the head the acceptance judge read, in the image the `gates` job runs in: 8 passed. These were self-tests (1288 cases with 1207 red proofs), tree, knowledge, reference (232 rules, 661 cases, 0 errors), compiler-tests (3869 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, the corpus runner among them, which builds and runs the three output cases), unicode-tables, ci-config and push-hook. `merge-checks` FAILs, since the container has no forge login. `pr-title`, `pr-breadth` and `pr-findings` were NOT RUN, with no pull request yet.\n- The same run on 8e017da, the head with the fixes made after the judgment (named in the acceptance comment): 8 passed, with compiler-tests at 3871 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, and the other counts the same. `merge-checks` FAILs, and the three pull-request gates were NOT RUN.\n- An earlier run of the same change failed `tree`: a sample path in the form of a drive letter. That sample is now `r\"a\\nb\\tc\"`.\n- The four mutations of the \"Compiler\" section were each seen red.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
